  "source_P012": "So the error becomes small compared with the displacement. We abbreviate this property as $`R_1(h)=o(h)`$: the symbol $`o(h)`$ denotes an unspecified remainder whose ratio to $`h`$ tends to zero. It need not be zero, and it is not a stated error bound at a particular nonzero input. Writing $`f(x)\\approx L(x)`$ means using that local model. The guarantee concerns approach to $`x_0`$; it does not say the error must increase monotonically at every step away from $`x_0`$.",
